{{Short description|Two groups of Arabic consonants}}
{{Hatnote|In this article, the [[Arabic alphabet]] is [[Romanization of Arabic|transliterated]] according to [[ALA-LC]] romanization.}}
{{redirect|Moon letter|the adaptation of the Roman alphabet into tactile form|Moon type}}
[[File:Arabic Sun and moon letters.png|alt=The Arabic Sun consonants in White and Moon consonants in Black|thumb|363x363px|The Arabic Sun consonants in black on white and Moon consonants in white on black]]
[[File:Maltese sun and moon consonants.png|alt=The Maltese moon consonants highlighted in white on black, the sun consonants in black on white, and the vowels in gray|thumb|The Maltese moon consonants highlighted in white on black, the sun consonants in black on white, and the vowels in gray]]
In [[Modern Standard Arabic|Arabic]] and [[Maltese language|Maltese]], all [[consonant]]s are classified into two distinct groups known as '''sun letters''' ({{langx|ar|حروف شمسية}} ''{{transliteration|ar|ALA|ḥurūf shamsiyyah}}'', {{langx|mt|konsonanti xemxin}}) and '''moon letters''' ([[Arabic]]: {{lang|ar|حروف قمرية}} ''{{transliteration|ar|ALA|ḥurūf qamariyyah}}'', {{langx|mt|konsonanti qamrin}}).

This distinction affects the way the definite article (equivalent to "[[the]]" in English) is [[Assimilation (phonology)|assimilated]] or pronounced before consonants: when a word begins with a '''sun letter''', the definite article assimilates with the initial [[consonant]] of the word. 

The names stem from how the definite article interacts with the nouns "Sun" and "Moon" in Arabic (and Maltese). In Arabic, '''al-shams''' (“the Sun”) becomes '''ash-shams''' (assimilating the '''[[Lamedh#Arabic_lām|lām]]'''), while '''al-qamar''' (“the Moon”) remains unchanged. Similarly, in [[Maltese language|Maltese]], "the Sun" is '''ix-xemx''' (with [[Assimilation (phonology)|assimilation]]), while "the Moon" is '''il-qamar''' (without assimilation).

==Rule==
When followed by a sun letter, the {{IPAslink|l}} of the Arabic definite article {{Transliteration|ar|ALA|al-}} assimilates to the initial consonant of the following noun, resulting in a [[Gemination|doubled consonant]]. For example, "the Nile" is pronounced {{Transliteration|ar|an-Nīl}}, not {{Transliteration|ar|al-Nīl}}.

When the Arabic definite article (<span style="font-size:135%;">{{lang|ar|الْـ}}</span>) is followed by a moon letter, no assimilation takes place.

The sun letters represent the [[coronal consonant]]s according to the phonology of Classical Arabic, and the moon letters represent all others. Note that the mnemonic {{lang|ar|اِبْغِ حَجَّكَ وَخَفْ عَقِيمَهُ}} (pronounced somewhat like ''aibgh hajak wakhaf eaqimah'') groups all moon letters.

