===Jīm===
The letter <span style="font-size:135%;">{{lang|ar|[[Gimel|ج]]}}</span> {{Transliteration|ar|ALA|jīm}} is pronounced differently depending on the region of the speaker. In many regions it represents a coronal consonant such as {{IPAblink|d͡ʒ}} or {{IPAblink|ʒ}}. However, in [[Classical Arabic]], it represented a [[Palatalization (phonetics)|palatalized]] voiced velar plosive {{IPAslink|ɡʲ}} or a voiced palatal plosive {{IPAslink|ɟ}}. A contemporary pronunciation as {{IPAblink|ɡ}}<ref>{{Cite book |last=al Nassir |first=Abdulmunʿim Abdulamir |url=https://etheses.whiterose.ac.uk/10917/1/354409.pdf |title=Sibawayh the Phonologist |publisher=University of New York |year=1985 |pages=80 |language=ar |access-date=23 April 2024}}</ref> is retained in [[Egypt]], [[Oman]], and coastal [[Yemen]] or {{IPAblink|ɟ}} in eastern hinterland Yemen, and as a variant in Sudan. As a result, it was classified as a moon letter, and it does not assimilate the article in Classical Arabic. Maltese ġ {{IPA|/d͡ʒ/}} is also considered a moon consonant, whereas its voiceless counterpart ċ {{IPAslink|t͡ʃ}} is a sun consonant.

However, in some varieties of [[Moroccan Arabic|Moroccan]], [[Mesopotamian Arabic|Mesopotamian]], and [[Palestinian Arabic|Palestinian]] Arabic, {{Transliteration|ar|ALA|jīm}} (often /{{IPA|ʒ}}/) assimilates, like a sun letter, e.g., {{Transliteration|ar|ij-jamal}} 'camel'.<ref>{{cite book |last=Heath |first=Jeffrey |title=Jewish and Muslim Dialects of Moroccan Arabic |date= 2013 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-136-12642-0 |page=169 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=6cW_5DOhw-wC&pg=PA169 |quote=Although CA ''j''&nbsp;... was not a "sun" letter, its MA reflex 'ʾ normally is, hence {{Transliteration|ar|ž-žbəl}} 'the mountain' (CA {{Transliteration|ar|al-jabal}}). There are some exceptions in Jebli dialects.&nbsp;... borrowings from e.g. French normally assimilates, as in {{Transliteration|ar|ž-žuṛnal}} 'the newspaper'.}}</ref><ref name=PA/>

=== Emphatic consonants ===

In Arabic dialects, like Palestinian, ''al'' before an [[emphatic consonant]] only assimilates in place of articulation but not in pharyngealization, hence {{Transliteration|ar|it-ṭāwla}} instead of {{Transliteration|ar|aṭ-ṭāwila}} ({{wikt-lang|ar|طاولة|الطاولة}} 'table').<ref name=PA>{{cite book |last=Sirhan |first=N. |title=Folk Stories and Personal Narratives in Palestinian Spoken Arabic |date=2014 |publisher=Springer |isbn=978-1-137-32576-1 |page=91 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=aOCvAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA91 |quote= Although /j/ is not a sun letter, in PA it assimilates as though it were: {{Transliteration|ar|il-jamal}} – {{Transliteration|ar|ij-jamal}} (camel).  […] when an emphatic sun letter has a less emphatic counterpart (i.e. non-pharyngealised) […] the definite article assimilates in place of articulation but not in pharyngealisation. [..] {{Transliteration|ar|it-ṭāwla}} […] {{Transliteration|ar|is-ṣalātu}} }}</ref>

