If a word starts with any of the moon letters, the definite article il- stays the same and does not assimilate, while with the sun letters it assimilates accordingly to: iċ-, id-, in-, ir-, is-, it-, ix-, iż-, iz-. It is also worth mentioning that words starting with vowels, and the letters għ, and h get the definite article l- (minus the initial i). When the definite article comes exactly after a word ending in a vowel, the initial <nowiki><i> of the article always drops, as in "dak </nowiki>'''ir'''-raġel ra '''r'''-raġel" (that man saw the man). When a word starts with two consonants, the definite article used is l-, but an i is attached at the beginning of the word: skola > l-iskola and Żvezja > l-Iżvezja.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Aidan|title=The Definite Article in Maltese|url=https://malteseboy.tumblr.com/post/160044174253/the-definite-article-in-maltese-the-definite}}</ref>

The sound {{IPAslink|l}} (represented by the letters {{lang|mt|L}} and {{lang|ar|ل}}) function in the same way regardless of if it is a sun or moon letter, e.g. (the meat) is il-laħam in Maltese and {{lang|ar|الْلَحْمْ}} al-laḥm in Arabic or (the game) is il-logħba in Maltese and {{lang|ar|الْلُعْبَة}} al-lu[[ʿ]]<nowiki/>ba in Arabic.

==Orthography==

In the written language, the ⟨<span style="font-size:150%; line-height:1;">{{script/Arabic|ال}}</span>⟩ {{Transliteration|ar|ALA|al}} is retained regardless of how it is pronounced.<ref name="emsa">{{cite book |last=Abboud |first=Peter F. |url=https://archive.org/details/elementarymodern02abbo |title=Elementary Modern Standard Arabic 1 |publisher=Cambridge UP |year=1983 |isbn=0-521-27295-5 |pages=[https://archive.org/details/elementarymodern02abbo/page/n144 123]–124 |display-authors=etal |url-access=limited}}</ref> When full diacritics are used, assimilation may be expressed by putting a [[Shadda|''{{Transliteration|ar|ALA|shaddah}}'']] ⟨ ّ⟩ on the consonant after the {{Transliteration|ar|ALA-LC|lām}} ⟨<span style="font-size:150%; line-height:1;">{{script/Arabic|ل}}</span>⟩. Non-assimilation may be expressed by placing a ''[[Sukun|{{Transliteration|ar|ALA|sukūn}}]]'' over the {{Transliteration|ar|ALA-LC|lām}} ⟨<span style="font-size:150%; line-height:1;">{{script/Arabic|لْ}}</span>⟩.

Most modern-written Arabic names (including personal names and geographical Arabic names) do not follow the consonant assimilation rule or the shaddah when Latinized in Latin-spelled languages. Sometimes the sun and moon rules are not followed in casual speech. They are also mostly spaced rather than hyphenated.

E.g. personal name:
* <span style="font-size:150%;">{{script/Arabic|الرَّحْمَن}}</span><!--THIS WORD ISN'T SPELLED WITH ALEF. IT'S EXCEPTIONAL--> - ''Al Rahman'' or ''El Rahman'' instead of ''Ar-Raḥmān''

transliterated geographical name:
* <span style="font-size:150%;">{{script/Arabic|الْجُمْهُورِيَّةُ التُّونِسِيَّة}}</span> - ''Al Jumhuriyah Al Tunisiyah'' instead of ''al-Jumhūrīyatu t-Tūnisīyah''

